#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:2
msgid "``shell`` tasks"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:4
msgid ""
"**Shell tasks** are similar to simple command tasks except that they are "
"executed inside a new shell, and so are interpreted as a shell script "
"instead of a single command. This means they can leverage the full syntax of "
"the shell interpreter such as command substitution, pipes, background "
"processes, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:6
msgid ""
"An example use case for this might be opening some ssh tunnels in the "
"background with one task and closing them with another like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:8
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.pfwd]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"\n"
"  ssh -N -L 0.0.0.0:8080:$STAGING:8080 $STAGING &\n"
"  ssh -N -L 0.0.0.0:5432:$STAGINGDB:5432 $STAGINGDB &\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"\n"
"[tool.poe.tasks.pfwdstop]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"kill $(pgrep -f \"ssh -N -L .*:(8080|5432)\")\"\"\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:21
msgid ""
"By default poe attempts to find a POSIX shell (sh, bash, or zsh in that "
"order) on the system and uses that. When running on Windows, poe will first "
"look for |git_bash_link| at the usual location, and otherwise attempt to "
"find it via the PATH, though this might not always be possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:89
msgid "<a href=\"https://gitforwindows.org\" target=\"_blank\">git bash</a>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:25
msgid "Available task options"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:27
msgid ""
"``shell`` tasks support all of the :doc:`standard task options <../options>` "
"with the exception of ``use_exec``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:29
msgid "The following options are also accepted:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:31
msgid ""
"**interpreter** : ``str`` | ``list[str]`` :ref:`📖<Using a different shell "
"interpreter>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:30
msgid "str | list[str] 📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:32
msgid ""
"Specify the shell interpreter that this task should execute with, or a list "
"of interpreters in order of preference."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:34
msgid ""
"**ignore_fail** : ``bool`` | ``list[int]``  :ref:`📖<Ignore task failure>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:33
msgid "bool | list[int]  📖"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:35
msgid ""
"Return exit code 0 even if the task fails, or specify a list of task exit "
"codes to ignore."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:39
msgid "Using a different shell interpreter"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:41
msgid ""
"It is also possible to specify an alternative interpreter (or list of "
"compatible interpreters ordered by preference) to be invoked to execute "
"shell task content. For example if you only expect the task to be executed "
"on Windows or other environments with PowerShell installed then you can "
"specify a PowerShell-based task like so:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:43
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.install-poetry]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"\n"
"(Invoke-WebRequest -Uri https://raw.githubusercontent.com/python-poetry/"
"poetry/master/get-poetry.py -UseBasicParsing).Content | python -\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"interpreter = \"pwsh\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:51
msgid ""
"If your task content is restricted to syntax that is valid for both POSIX "
"shells and PowerShell then you can maximise the likelihood of it working on "
"any system by specifying the interpreter as:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:53
msgid "interpreter = [\"posix\", \"pwsh\"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:57
msgid ""
"It is also possible to specify python code as the shell task code as in the "
"following example. However it is recommended to use a :doc:`script<script>` "
"or :doc:`expr<expr>` task rather than writing complex code inline within "
"your pyproject.toml."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:59
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.time]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"\n"
"from datetime import datetime\n"
"\n"
"print(datetime.now())\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"interpreter = \"python\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:69
msgid "The following interpreter values may be used:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:71
msgid "posix"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:72
msgid ""
"This is the default behavior, equivalent to ``[\"sh\", \"bash\", \"zsh\"]``, "
"meaning that poe will try to find sh, and fallback to bash, then zsh."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:73
msgid "sh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:74
msgid ""
"Use the basic POSIX shell. This is often an alias for either bash or dash "
"depending on the operating system."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:75
msgid "bash"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:76
msgid ""
"Uses whatever version of bash can be found. This is usually the most "
"portable option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:77
msgid "zsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:78
msgid "Uses whatever version of zsh can be found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:79
msgid "fish"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:80
msgid "Uses whatever version of fish can be found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:81
msgid "pwsh"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:82
msgid "Uses powershell version 6 or higher."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:83
msgid "powershell"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:84
msgid "Uses the newest version of powershell that can be found."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:86
msgid ""
"The default value can be changed with the global ``shell_interpreter`` "
"option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:95
msgid "Task arguments"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:97
msgid ""
"As with other task types, shell tasks support configuring named arguments "
"via the ``args`` option. Argument values are set as environment variables on "
"the shell subprocess."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:99
msgid ""
"[tool.poe.tasks.greet]\n"
"shell = \"\"\"\n"
"echo \"${GREETING} ${NAME}\"\n"
"echo \"How are you?\"\n"
"\"\"\"\n"
"args = [\n"
"  { name = \"NAME\", default = \"world\" },\n"
"  { name = \"GREETING\", default = \"Hi\" },\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:111
msgid ""
"See the :doc:`args guide<../../guides/args_guide>` for full details on "
"configuring arguments."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:115
msgid ""
"Unlike cmd, script, or expr tasks, shell tasks only access variables from "
"the environment at runtime via the shell interpreter. Therefore ``_private`` "
"environment variables cannot be accessed from shell task content, unless "
"they are remapped to a normal environment variable via the ``env`` option."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:119
msgid "Accessing free arguments via ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:121
msgid ""
"Free arguments (arguments not matched by any named arg definition, or "
"arguments passed after :sh:`--`) are available inside shell task scripts as "
"the ``$POE_EXTRA_ARGS`` environment variable. The value is a shell-quoted, "
"space-delimited string, ready to be expanded inline."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../tasks/task_types/shell.rst:123
msgid ""
"See the :ref:`forwarding-free-arguments-via-poe-extra-args` section of the "
"args guide for details and examples."
msgstr ""
